1
00:00:05,246 --> 00:00:10,250
Every laboratory that runs a control material has to decide where to put the limits.

2
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:16,158
Put them wide and nothing ever fails: the chart looks calm, but a real shift would pass unnoticed.

3
00:00:16,208 --> 00:00:20,603
Put them tight and the chart flags something every week, most of it noise.

4
00:00:20,653 --> 00:00:23,161
The question is not whether to have limits.

5
00:00:23,211 --> 00:00:24,805
It is where they come from.

6
00:00:27,681 --> 00:00:32,085
Most limits are taken from the control manufacturer's assigned range.

7
00:00:32,135 --> 00:00:36,931
That range is set to cover many laboratories, many analysers and many lots.

8
00:00:36,981 --> 00:00:38,437
It is deliberately wide.

9
00:00:38,487 --> 00:00:48,327
On our own analysers, the range defined in the laboratory system was between one and a half and seven times wider than the scatter we actually observed.

10
00:00:48,377 --> 00:00:54,614
A limit seven times wider than the noise cannot detect a shift of one or two standard deviations.

11
00:00:54,664 --> 00:00:55,858
It is not a control.

12
00:00:55,908 --> 00:00:57,037
it is a formality.

13
00:01:00,195 --> 00:01:08,944
The alternative starts with one number: the coefficient of variation your own analyser actually produces, on the control level you actually run.

14
00:01:08,994 --> 00:01:16,944
We compute it within each control lot, so that a change of target at a lot change does not inflate it, and we pool across the lots.

15
00:01:16,994 --> 00:01:20,513
Where three analysers share a rule, we take the highest CV.

16
00:01:20,563 --> 00:01:26,235
That is the conservative choice: a rule that works on the worst analyser works on the others.

17
00:01:29,314 --> 00:01:35,789
The second number is the allowable total error: how much a result may be wrong before it matters clinically.

18
00:01:35,839 --> 00:01:44,265
This is a specification, not a measurement, and it comes from published sources: biological variation, external quality schemes, regulators.

19
00:01:44,315 --> 00:01:48,594
Dividing it by your CV gives the allowable error in standard deviations.

20
00:01:48,644 --> 00:01:50,058
This is the sigma metric.

21
00:01:50,108 --> 00:01:56,826
It says how much room there is between the noise of the method and the point where the error becomes a problem.

22
00:01:59,931 --> 00:02:01,864
Not all of that room is available.

23
00:02:01,914 --> 00:02:11,841
If the method shifts, five per cent of results will exceed the allowable error once the shift reaches the allowable error minus one point six five standard deviations.

24
00:02:11,891 --> 00:02:16,408
That is the critical shift: the smallest shift the control rule has to catch.

25
00:02:16,458 --> 00:02:22,839
If it comes out negative, no rule can catch it, because the noise alone already exceeds the allowable error.

26
00:02:22,889 --> 00:02:27,827
That is what happens to MCHC under the tightest biological-variation specification.

27
00:02:30,965 --> 00:02:36,821
A rule is a limit at K standard deviations and a count n of control results per event.

28
00:02:36,871 --> 00:02:38,002
Two things follow.

29
00:02:38,052 --> 00:02:44,672
The chance the rule fires when nothing is wrong, false rejection, P F R, depends only on K and n.

30
00:02:44,722 --> 00:02:53,009
The chance it fires when the critical shift has happened, error detection, P E D, depends on K, n and the critical shift.

31
00:02:53,059 --> 00:02:56,205
Tighten K and P E D goes up, but so does P F R.

32
00:02:56,255 --> 00:03:00,304
The whole design is choosing where on that trade-off to sit.

33
00:03:00,354 --> 00:03:08,294
We set a budget: false rejection under one and a half per cent per control event, combined across all three levels.

34
00:03:11,679 --> 00:03:16,243
The three levels are measured together, and any one of them can reject the run.

35
00:03:16,293 --> 00:03:17,900
So the probabilities combine.

36
00:03:17,950 --> 00:03:23,283
A rule that costs half a per cent at each level costs one and a half per cent for the event.

37
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:32,867
That is why the budget is set per event, not per level, and why a level with plenty of sigma can be run tighter, spending the budget where detection matters most.

38
00:03:35,786 --> 00:03:39,279
The result is an action limit in the units of the measurement.

39
00:03:39,329 --> 00:03:48,628
Most laboratory systems apply a fixed three-standard-deviation rule, so the last step is to give the system an SD such that three of them equal your action limit.

40
00:03:48,678 --> 00:03:52,461
From then on the system is enforcing your rule under its own name.

41
00:03:55,267 --> 00:04:00,538
One more thing, which we only saw when we applied the rules back to seven months of data.

42
00:04:00,588 --> 00:04:02,352
The limits sit around a target.

43
00:04:02,402 --> 00:04:12,390
If the target was set elsewhere, by the manufacturer, or by a single analyser, your own analysers sit off-centre, and a tight rule fires on the offset, not on a fault.

44
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:16,985
In our data the false rejection rate more than tripled for that reason alone.

45
00:04:17,035 --> 00:04:22,488
Setting the shared target to the pooled mean of the analysers brought it back within budget.

46
00:04:22,538 --> 00:04:24,302
The rule from your imprecision.

47
00:04:24,352 --> 00:04:25,814
the target from your mean.

48
00:04:25,864 --> 00:04:27,325
Both, from the same data.
